# Astro Component Guidelines

Rules for writing consistent Astro components for the ONE framework.

## Structure
- Use the standard Astro component structure with frontmatter at the top.
- Keep component logic in the frontmatter section.
- Use TypeScript for type safety.

## Styling
- Use Tailwind CSS for styling.
- Avoid inline styles; prefer Tailwind classes.
- Use `class` for Astro components and `className` for React components.

## Props
- Define props with TypeScript interfaces.
- Use destructuring for props in the frontmatter.
- Provide default values for optional props.

## Example
Here's a compliant Astro component:

```astro
---
// Define props with TypeScript
interface Props {
  title: string;
  description?: string;
}

// Destructure props with defaults
const { title, description = "Default description" } = Astro.props;
---

<div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-white shadow-md">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-primary">{title}</h2>
  {description && <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">{description}</p>}
  <slot />
</div>
```

## React Islands
- Use client directives appropriately: `client:load`, `client:idle`, `client:visible`.
- Keep React components small and focused.
- Pass data to React components via props, not global state when possible.

## Performance
- Minimize client-side JavaScript.
- Use static rendering when possible.
- Leverage Astro's partial hydration model.
- Optimize images using Astro's built-in image optimization.

## Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML elements.
- Include proper ARIA attributes when needed.
- Ensure proper color contrast with Tailwind.
- Test with keyboard navigation.

## File Organization
- Place components in `src/components/`.
- Use subdirectories for related components.
- Follow naming conventions: PascalCase for component files. 